ref 17592186179648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050310 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050310 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429224 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050310 #edb/ref 1031 "Adding Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050310 #edb/ref 1033 3739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050310 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3ef53278-7672-5742-99fe-d07cf78639fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061945 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061945 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061945 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061945 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061945 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061945 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061945 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061945 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061945 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1b873f0e-d503-59f0-bb98-854722b44541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064143 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064143 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the derivative of the function $y = x^{x^3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064143 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a369d9b2-7637-5339-bd75-5f49b1aac962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064143 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = x^{x^3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{{x}^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+({x}^{3})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=x^{x^3}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{{x}^{3}}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{{x}^{3}}({x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)) \\\\  & ={x}^{{x}^{3}+2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064144 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064144 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the derivative of the function $y = (x^2-1)^x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064144 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bdccebc1-05df-5c7b-a57b-fa50df42a32e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064144 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = (x^2-1)^x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}-1)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\cdot 2x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = (x^2-1)^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =({x}^{2}-1)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)^{x}(\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)}{{x}^{2}-1}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)^{x-1}(({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064145 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9844" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064145 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the derivative of the function $y = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064145 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d047c1a-308b-553b-89e4-9afc5cc9f766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064145 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1039 "Differentiating X Raised to the Power of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064306 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "44425e0c-c9f0-5589-a298-071f456fd0c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1039 "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of X" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064307 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6cc92652-6746-583d-bb0e-53991d5f5e1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1039 "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064308 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "94a2b8cb-aca4-5e18-bb5c-71c0738c9e30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064398 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064398 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064398 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064398 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064398 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064398 #edb/ref 1045 "Logarithmic Differentiation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064398 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064398 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064398 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064398 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "2c535ceb-c030-5aed-b263-486be04ce20c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064648 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064648 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064648 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064648 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $y=(2x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(2x)^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064648 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e49f218d-0bc2-5311-beb0-0c74453b8fc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064648 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(2x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x}\\cdot 2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=y=(2x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(2x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065202 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065202 #edb/ref 1061 37025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065202 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9aa16304-115f-5e48-8777-8b493acf1b51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065202 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065203 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065203 #edb/ref 1061 37026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065203 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5b0b9f01-a558-5a72-80f9-542d588ba830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065203 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065204 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065204 #edb/ref 1061 37027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065204 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ed78f46-95a3-57ea-b05a-7c9ceb493d71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065204 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065205 #edb/ref 1061 37028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065205 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8d9521b5-8275-530d-9b3c-7e382a4d4606" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065205 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065395 #edb/ref 1064 "**Logarithmic differentiation** is a technique to compute derivatives that are difficult to find using the usual rules of differentiation, such as the product, quotient, and chain rules.\n\nLet's consider the following function.\n\n$$\ny = x^x\n$$\n\nWe aim to compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$ However, in this case, we note the following:\n\n- Both the base and the exponent are functions of $x.$\n\n- This means we cannot apply the power rule since the exponent is not a fixed number.\n\n- We also cannot apply the rules for differentiating exponentials since the base is not a fixed number.\n\nSo instead, we'll use logarithmic differentiation. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We take the natural logarithm of both sides and apply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$ using implicit differentiation and the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Now, we solve for $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  y\\left( \\ln x + 1 \\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Finally, we substitute back our original expression for $y=x^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^x \\left( \\ln x + 1 \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065395 #edb/ref 1065 6087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065395 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "21321352-0483-513c-828f-f1c46306015a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065395 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410216 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x{tan}^{2}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410216 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410216 #edb/ref 1083 #u